 The b12x MoE backend does not support expert parallelism, expert maps, EXL3, or NF3.
 
+## Per-phase activations
+
+NVFP4 W4A16 linears keep BF16 activations by default, and MXFP8 linears pick
+BF16 or MXFP8 activations by call size. `quantization_config.linear.phase`
+sets the activations of decode and prefill calls instead, for example to
+quantize long prefills to NVFP4 and run the FP4 GEMM. This is lossy, so check
+accuracy for your model:
+
+```bash
+vllm serve <model> \
+    --linear-backend b12x \
+    --quantization-config '{"linear": {"phase": {"decode": "unquantized",
+        "prefill": "nvfp4", "max_decode_tokens": 1024}}}'
+```
+
+See [Separate decode and prefill activations](online.md#separate-decode-and-prefill-activations)
+for the supported formats and constraints. Phases need BF16 activations
+(`--dtype bfloat16`). `phase` raises an error when a linear it applies to
+cannot serve it. It does not apply to `draft_lm_head_quantization` lm_head
+copies, which keep BF16 activations.
+
+For most layers, linear input rows are the tokens scheduled in the step. NVFP4
+calls are quantized with one global scale per call, computed from the absolute
+maximum of its real rows and shared by every request in the step, so outputs
+depend on batch composition, and a non-finite activation in one request makes
+the outputs and KV cache of every request in that step non-finite, including
+prefix-cache blocks computed in it. Client-supplied prompt and multimodal
+embeddings are therefore not supported with NVFP4 phases. Smaller
+`max_decode_tokens` values can slow down mid-size prefills. NVFP4 layers whose
+per-GPU input size is not a multiple of 128 keep BF16 activations.
+
